WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.556
A field permits addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division by every nonzero element

00:00:06.556 --> 00:00:09.120
Rational numbers have this property

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:18.867
Zero remains excluded from division: multiplying zero by any candidate inverse still gives zero, never the required identity one.

00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:22.208
Finite fields are possible too

00:00:22.208 --> 00:00:28.333
Modulo the prime five, three has inverse two because their product six leaves remainder one

00:00:28.333 --> 00:00:34.867
To solve three x equals four modulo five, multiply by two and reduce eight to three.

00:00:36.167 --> 00:00:41.151
Check the result directly: three times three is nine, with remainder four

00:00:41.151 --> 00:00:44.890
Modulo a prime, every nonzero remainder has an inverse

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:52.367
For composite moduli that statement can fail, so being a remainder system alone does not guarantee a field.

00:00:53.667 --> 00:00:58.433
A field also determines which polynomial equations have solutions within it

00:00:58.433 --> 00:01:02.767
The real numbers contain no square root of negative one

00:01:02.767 --> 00:01:09.700
Extending to complex numbers adds i and its negative as roots, while keeping valid field arithmetic.
